  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 304 AM EDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\n- Rain ending Friday\n\nNational radar shows a large band of rainfall from Michigan\nsouthwest to the southern Plains, moving northeast. The rain is\nahead of a cold front that will move across the cwa Friday. Deep\nsouth flow is in place east of the front and is drawing moisture\nnorthward from the Gulf as well as from the eastern Pacific, where\nthe remnants of the tropical system have been entrained into the\nupper flow. This added moisture has resulted in flooding in the\nMid MS Valley. However, flooding isn't expected within the cwa.\nLight to occasionally moderate rainfall will continue into the\novernight. Additional rainfall accumulations of a half to around\nan inch are possible. Once low pressure over Kansas moves \nnortheast along the frontal boundary into Michigan, the cold front\nwill move southeast across Lower MI beginning tonight and \ncontinuing into Friday. As high pressure builds in behind it, \nwe'll see decreasing cloudiness throughout the day. Dry weather \nwill then continue through the weekend and much of next week.\n\n- Cooler with frost/freeze possible into next week\n\nClear skies and light winds will set up radiational cooling\nconditions. Temperatures in the upper 20s are possible along US-10\nwith lower to mid 30s as far south as M-20. Thus, frost/freeze\nheadlines are likely tomorrow night. After another cold frontal\npassage Sunday, additional frost/freeze headlines are possible\nSunday night and Monday night. Monday night will be the coldest of\nthe season so far. Lows Monday night will be in the mid to upper\n20s north of M-20 and in the lower to mid 30s south through the\nrest of the cwa. Highs during this period will be in the lower to\nmid 60s.",
